        note: 'Average 104 days across 2013–2024, range 96–113. Amazon takes about three and a half months to pay suppliers after receiving goods — among the highest in retail and tech, where most competitors pay in 30–60 days (Walmart ~40–50, Target ~60). By delaying payment this long Amazon effectively uses suppliers as a low- or zero-cost source of financing, funding inventory, operations and growth without borrowing. In 2024, ~$94bn of accounts payable at 106 days means roughly $94bn of interest-free supplier financing at any moment. The consistently high DPO reflects enormous purchasing power, an efficient supply chain that can absorb extended terms without disruption, and disciplined payment timing that maximises float without damaging relationships. 2020 peaked at 113 days on pandemic leverage; 2021–2024 stable at 101–106.'
