      dpo: { label: 'Days Payable Outstanding', unit: 'days', series: [ {year:2017,value:29}, {year:2018,value:24}, {year:2019,value:27}, {year:2020,value:29}, {year:2021,value:35}, {year:2022,value:54}, {year:2023,value:32}, {year:2024,value:57}, {year:2025,value:110} ],
        note: 'The historic norm through FY2017–FY2021 was 25–35 days — very conservative, Oracle paid suppliers relatively quickly. Then a structural shift: FY2023–FY2025 rise sharply, with FY2025 at ~110 days a huge jump. This reflects data-centre buildouts, large hardware and infrastructure vendors, extended payment terms and strong bargaining power — Oracle is using suppliers to finance growth. Not bad in itself, provided operating cash flow remains strong (it is), suppliers stay stable and diversified (they are), and no payment disputes emerge (worth monitoring). Typical of cloud infrastructure builders and hyperscaler-style economics.'
      },
